(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-i"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form I","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which organism do the first generation cephalosporins fail to cover?","choices":["Escherichia coli","Proteus species","Enterococcus","Klebsiella species"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They do cover some Escherichia coli, Proteus and Klebsiella. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is isoproterenol used for?","choices":["Chronic maintenance of asthma","Stimulating the heart in an emergency","Raising blood pressure in septic shock","Nasal decongestion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as a beta-1 and beta-2 agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Atropine is the antidote for which poisonings?","choices":["Tricyclic antidepressant overdose","Beta blocker overdose","Organophosphate poisoning needing enzyme reactivation","Cholinesterase inhibitor insecticides and some mushrooms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, opposing the cholinergic excess. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which anti-herpes prodrug has 77 percent bioavailability?","choices":["Famciclovir","Penciclovir","Valacyclovir","Valganciclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, against 5 percent for intravenous penciclovir. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"What is missing from the natural penicillin spectrum?","choices":["Anaerobes of every kind, including Bacteroides","Gram-positive cocci, which need a broader agent","Staphylococcus and aerobic Gram-negative organisms","Streptococci, which require a cephalosporin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anaerobic activity is present, except against Bacteroides. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What trough is targeted for gentamicin and tobramycin?","choices":["Less than 5","Less than 1","Between 10 and 15","Between 15 and 20"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while amikacin is held below 5. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How does norepinephrine compare with epinephrine at blood vessels?","choices":["It produces greater vasodilation","It has no vascular effect at all","It produces greater vasoconstriction","It acts mainly on beta-2 receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, acting mainly through alpha-1 receptors. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What does propranolol do to the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes?","choices":["Stimulates both, producing tachycardia","Affects the atrioventricular node only","Leaves conduction unchanged","Depresses both, producing bradycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as a negative inotrope and chronotrope. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"Why do the noncatecholamines last longer?","choices":["Catechol-O-methyltransferase does not inactivate them","They bind receptors irreversibly","They are stored in vesicles for later release","They are excreted unchanged by the kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are poor substrates for monoamine oxidase. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the major inhibitory one in the brain?","choices":["Glutamate","Glycine","Gamma-aminobutyric acid","Dopamine in the basal ganglia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while glutamate is the most common excitatory one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Penicillin G is the drug of choice for which infections?","choices":["Syphilis, gas gangrene and meningococcus","Diabetic foot, animal bites and cellulitis","Nosocomial pneumonia and intra-abdominal sepsis","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It has good Gram-positive coccal activity but no Staphylococcus coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is tigecycline dosed?","choices":["50 milligrams once, then 100 milligrams every 12 hours","100 milligrams every 12 hours without a load","100 milligrams once, then 50 milligrams every 12 hours","1 gram once daily by intravenous infusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, infused over 30 to 60 minutes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the ceftriaxone dose for meningitis?","choices":["2 grams intravenously every 12 hours","1 gram intravenously every 24 hours","500 milligrams intravenously every 6 hours","4 grams intravenously every 8 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, against a usual range of 1 to 2 grams every 12 to 24 hours. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Topical retinoids are first line for which type of acne?","choices":["Nodular acne with scarring","Drug-induced acne from steroids","Non-inflammatory comedonal acne","Acne with secondary infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are combined with other agents for inflammatory disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which tick-borne illnesses do the tetracyclines treat?","choices":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever and Lyme disease","Malaria and babesiosis in returning travellers","Typhoid fever and paratyphoid infection","Leptospirosis and brucellosis only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside acne and atypical pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which epinephrine effect predominates at low dose?","choices":["Alpha effects, producing vasoconstriction","Neither; the effects cancel out","Beta effects, producing vasodilation","Muscarinic effects on the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while alpha vasoconstriction predominates at high dose. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which atropine isomer relaxes the gastrointestinal tract and bladder?","choices":["Hyoscine","Hyoscyamine","Glycopyrronium","Dicyclomine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, used for its antispasmodic action. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Where is tyramine found?","choices":["Citrus fruit and grapefruit juice","Fermented foods such as cheese and wine","Dairy products containing calcium","Green leafy vegetables high in vitamin K"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it has no therapeutic use of its own. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"How do the tetracyclines block translation?","choices":["They bind 23S ribosomal RNA of the 50S subunit","They block transpeptidation at the 50S subunit","They inhibit the formation of the 70S complex","They bind 16S ribosomal RNA of the 30S subunit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, preventing transfer RNA from binding the A site. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What does benzoyl peroxide become after crossing the stratum corneum?","choices":["Salicylic acid","Benzoic acid","Azelaic acid","Retinoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and that is what acts against Propionibacterium acnes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is pilocarpine used in xerostomia?","choices":["It blocks salivary muscarinic receptors","It increases mucous membrane blood flow","It reduces intraocular pressure","It potently stimulates sweat, tears and saliva"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is what relieves the dry mouth. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What advantage does dobutamine have over other sympathomimetics?","choices":["It has no effect on atrioventricular conduction","It can be given by mouth","Tolerance never develops with prolonged use","It does not greatly raise myocardial oxygen demand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which suits inotropic support after cardiac surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How effective is ciclopirox nail lacquer for onychomycosis?","choices":["About half of patients respond","Around 80 percent effective","Effectively curative in most cases","Less than 12 percent effective"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why it disappoints in practice. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do bacterial lipases do in acne?","choices":["Break down keratin in the follicular wall","Digest the sebaceous gland membrane","Convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone","Hydrolyse triglycerides into free fatty acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which increases keratinisation and microcomedone formation. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How do the polymyxins disrupt the Gram-negative membrane?","choices":["They form pores by binding ergosterol","They block the synthesis of lipopolysaccharide","They displace magnesium and calcium as a detergent","They inhibit the 30S ribosomal subunit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, interacting with the outer membrane lipopolysaccharide. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which topical aminoglycoside frequently causes sensitisation?","choices":["Gentamicin","Neomycin","Bacitracin","Mupirocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and systemic accumulation can occur with this group. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What makes the carbapenems unusually broad?","choices":["A completely different cellular target","Combination with a beta-lactamase inhibitor","Greater resistance to beta-lactamase hydrolysis","Concentration-dependent killing with a long effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why they suit extended spectrum producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which azole did itraconazole replace?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Fluconazole","Voriconazole","Posaconazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because itraconazole has a wider spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"How can corticosteroid resistance be overcome?","choices":["By switching to a more drying vehicle","By applying it less frequently","By combining it with an antibiotic","By increasing the concentration applied"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since a steeper gradient increases drug transfer. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which agent treats overdose of atropine or a tricyclic antidepressant?","choices":["Pralidoxime","Physostigmine","Neostigmine","Edrophonium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it also lowers intraocular pressure. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How do bacteria resist penicillin?","choices":["The ribosomal target is methylated","An efflux pump removes the drug","Beta-lactamase cleaves the ring","Porin channels close to block entry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is why beta-lactamase inhibitors were developed. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why has polymyxin resistance stayed uncommon?","choices":["The class went largely unused for 50 years","The target cannot be modified by bacteria","It is always given with a second agent","Resistance is lethal to the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and use is expected to rise with resistance elsewhere. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"When does transient hearing loss occur with a macrolide?","choices":["Only after prolonged oral therapy","Large intravenous doses or renal impairment","When combined with an antiarrhythmic","In children given the estolate salt"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the effect is described as transient. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How is norepinephrine extravasation treated?","choices":["Dantrolene given intravenously","A local beta blocker injection","Phentolamine, an alpha antagonist","Warm compresses alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because extreme vasoconstriction causes the damage. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which antibiotics bind the 30S ribosomal subunit?","choices":["Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines","Macrolides and clindamycin","Chloramphenicol and streptogramins","Fluoroquinolones and metronidazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides, clindamycin and chloramphenicol bind 50S. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What must a patient be told after applying a scopolamine patch?","choices":["Wash the hands thoroughly afterwards","Avoid all sunlight for 24 hours","Take it only with a fatty meal","Remove it before sleeping"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because touching the eye afterwards blurs vision. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why is dobutamine used cautiously in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["It speeds atrioventricular conduction","It causes profound bradycardia","It lowers blood pressure sharply","It prolongs the QT interval"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which risks a faster ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which enzyme does oseltamivir inhibit?","choices":["Neuraminidase","Haemagglutinin","Viral thymidine kinase","Reverse transcriptase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, preventing budding progeny from being cleaved free. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"What is bethanechol used to treat?","choices":["Glaucoma by lowering intraocular pressure","Postpartum or postoperative atonic bladder","Myasthenia gravis in chronic management","Xerostomia from reduced saliva"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, by stimulating the detrusor and relaxing the sphincter. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin needs no dose adjustment in renal impairment?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Ceftazidime","Cefepime","Ceftaroline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also carries good streptococcal coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does aztreonam cover?","choices":["Gram-positive organisms only","Anaerobes and Gram-positive aerobes","Broad coverage of all three groups","Gram-negative organisms only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with a spectrum resembling the aminoglycosides. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How are the cephalosporins mostly eliminated?","choices":["Hepatically, through bile","Renally","By plasma esterases","Unchanged through the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they also penetrate cerebrospinal fluid well. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which isotretinoin effects involve the skin and its appendages?","choices":["Hypopigmentation and skin atrophy","Acanthosis nigricans and multiple skin tags","Bleaching of hair and clothing","Retinoid dermatitis, alopecia and brittle nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside arthralgia and headaches. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which adverse effects follow albuterol?","choices":["Lethargy, sedation and dry mouth","Tremor, restlessness, apprehension and anxiety","Dizziness and nasal congestion","Bradycardia with bronchoconstriction and wheeze"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as a selective beta-2 agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which local adverse effect of topical steroids relates to the vehicle?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Growth retardation in children","Allergic dermatitis","Cataracts and glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside skin atrophy, acne and rosacea locally. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How long does isotretinoin take to work?","choices":["Six to eight weeks","Two to three weeks","One to three months","Six to twelve months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and serum lipids and mood need monitoring throughout. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What characterises concentration-dependent killing?","choices":["Killing plateaus once a concentration is reached","Higher concentration gives greater killing","Time above the inhibitory concentration matters most","Continuous infusion produces the best result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it comes with a post-antibiotic effect. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is azelaic acid thought to work?","choices":["Conversion to benzoic acid in the skin","Correcting abnormal follicular keratinisation","Inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis by chelating calcium","Antimicrobial action and blocking testosterone conversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though the mechanism is not fully understood. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does propranolol help after myocardial infarction?","choices":["It dissolves the occluding thrombus","It dilates the coronary arteries directly","It raises cardiac output to perfuse the muscle","It prevents a second infarct and reduces infarct size"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and lowers sudden death from arrhythmia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What can follow intralesional steroid injection for an acne nodule?","choices":["Permanent hypopigmentation of the whole face","Immediate rebound nodule formation","Adrenal suppression and local tissue atrophy","Tendon rupture at distant sites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because systemic absorption can occur. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which severe skin reactions are listed for the aminopenicillins?","choices":["Erythema nodosum and pyoderma gangrenosum","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Acanthosis nigricans and necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum","Pemphigus vulgaris and bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside interstitial nephritis and Clostridium difficile infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does succinylcholine suit rapid sequence intubation?","choices":["Its very long duration of paralysis","Rapid onset with short duration of action","It requires no monitoring of ventilation","It has no cardiovascular effects at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is used in the emergency department. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Into how many systems is the autonomic efferent division split?","choices":["Two: sympathetic and parasympathetic only","Two: somatic and autonomic","Three: enteric, parasympathetic and sympathetic","Four, including the somatic system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with the enteric division as the third. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the voriconazole intravenous vehicle, and why does it matter?","choices":["Cyclodextrin, which accumulates in renal failure","Deoxycholate, which drives infusion reactions","Polyethylene glycol, which irritates the skin","Saline alone, with no vehicle concern"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so renal function affects the intravenous form. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which linezolid interaction produces serotonin syndrome?","choices":["Warfarin through cytochrome 2C9 inhibition","Iron and calcium through chelation","Alcohol through a disulfiram-like reaction","Serotonin reuptake inhibitors and tyramine foods"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and pseudoephedrine is named alongside them. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which organisms are outside macrolide coverage?","choices":["Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae and pyogenes","Chlamydophila and Chlamydia trachomatis","Enterococci and most other Gram-negatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Haemophilus, Moraxella and Neisseria are covered. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why is penicillin V potassium the oral choice?","choices":["It has a much longer half-life","It penetrates the central nervous system","It avoids first-pass hepatic metabolism","It is stable in stomach acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin G is given intravenously instead. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What are ipratropium and tiotropium used for?","choices":["Preventing motion sickness by patch","Reducing bladder contraction frequency","Inhaled maintenance of bronchospasm in COPD","Treating glaucoma by lowering pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as synthetic antimuscarinic agents. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What happens with physostigmine at high doses?","choices":["Convulsions and muscle paralysis","Tachycardia and hypertension","Mydriasis and dry mouth","Malignant hyperthermia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, the paralysis coming from acetylcholine accumulation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What do the polymyxin black box warnings cover?","choices":["Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, neuromuscular blockade","QT prolongation and torsades de pointes","Bone marrow suppression and severe hepatotoxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is part of why they were set aside. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"}]};
